```javascript
function isPrime(n) {
  // Base cases
  if (n <= 1) {
    return false;
  }
  if (n <= 3) {
    return true;
  }

  // Check divisibility by 2 and 3
  if (n % 2 === 0 || n % 3 === 0) {
    return false;
  }

  // Check divisibility by numbers of the form 6k ± 1, up to sqrt(n)
  let i = 5;
  while (i * i <= n) {
    if (n % i === 0 || n % (i + 2) === 0) {
      return false;
    }
    i += 6;
  }

  return true;
}
```

This implementation follows the basic primality testing algorithm, where we check if a number is divisible by any number from 2 to the square root of the number. However, instead of using a loop, we increment the divisor `i` by 6 in each iteration. This is because all primes greater than 3 can be expressed in the form `6k ± 1`, where `k` is an integer. Therefore, we only need to check divisibility by numbers of this form.

