Here's a code in JavaScript that sorts an array of integers into descending order using the Merge Sort algorithm:

```javascript
function mergeSort(arr) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) {
    return arr;
  }

  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
  const left = arr.slice(0, mid);
  const right = arr.slice(mid);

  return merge(mergeSort(left), mergeSort(right));
}

function merge(left, right) {
  let result = [];
  let i = 0;
  let j = 0;

  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i] > right[j]) {
      result.push(left[i]);
      i++;
    } else {
      result.push(right[j]);
      j++;
    }
  }

  while (i < left.length) {
    result.push(left[i]);
    i++;
  }

  while (j < right.length) {
    result.push(right[j]);
    j++;
  }

  return result;
}

function sortDescending(arr) {
  const sortedArr = mergeSort(arr);
  return sortedArr.reverse();
}

// Test cases
console.log(sortDescending([4, 2, 9, 6, 5])); // Output: [9, 6, 5, 4, 2]
console.log(sortDescending([10, 2, 8, 6, 3])); // Output: [10, 8, 6, 3, 2]
console.log(sortDescending([5, 10, 15, 20])); // Output: [20, 15, 10, 5]
console.log(sortDescending([1])); // Output: [1]
console.log(sortDescending([])); // Output: []
```

Explanation:
1. The `mergeSort` function recursively splits the array into two halves until each half has only one element. It then merges the sorted halves together using the `merge` function.
2. The `merge` function merges two sorted arrays by comparing the elements at each index and pushing the larger element into the `result` array.
3. The `sortDescending` function calls the `mergeSort` function to get the sorted array in ascending order, and then reverses the array to get the final result in descending order.
4. The code handles edge cases such as an empty array or an array with a single element.
5. The time complexity of the code is O(n log n) since the merge sort algorithm has that time complexity. The space complexity is O(n) since we are creating new arrays for each recursion.

