To implement the iterative solution, we can use nested loops to compare each element in the list with every other element. If a duplicate is found, we return true; otherwise, we return false.

```python
def contains_duplicates_iterative(lst):
    n = len(lst)
    for i in range(n):
        for j in range(i+1, n):
            if lst[i] == lst[j]:
                return True
    return False
```

Time Complexity: The nested loops iterate through all possible pairs of elements in the list, resulting in a time complexity of O(n^2), where n is the length of the list.

Space Complexity: The iterative solution has a constant space complexity of O(1), as it does not use any additional data structures.

Recursive Solution:
To implement the recursive solution, we can use a helper function that checks if the first element in the list appears in the rest of the list. If it does, we return true; otherwise, we recursively call the helper function with the rest of the list.

```python
def contains_duplicates_recursive(lst):
    if len(lst) <= 1:
        return False
    elif lst[0] in lst[1:]:
        return True
    else:
        return contains_duplicates_recursive(lst[1:])
```

Time Complexity: In the worst case, where there are no duplicates, the recursive solution will perform n-1 comparisons, resulting in a time complexity of O(n), where n is the length of the list. However, in the best case, where a duplicate is found early in the list, the time complexity can be as low as O(1).

Space Complexity: The recursive solution has a space complexity of O(n), as each recursive call creates a new stack frame, consuming memory.

