In Java, deep copy and shallow copy are two different mechanisms used to create copies of objects. 

1. Shallow Copy: Shallow copy creates a new object and copies the reference of the original object to the new object. Both the original and copied objects refer to the same memory location, so any changes made to the object will be reflected in both the original and copied objects. 

Here's an example code demonstrating shallow copy:

```java
class Student {
    private int id;
    private String name;

    public Student(int id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Student [id=" + id + ", name=" + name + "]";
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student original = new Student(1, "John");
        Student copy = original;

        System.out.println(original);  // Student [id=1, name=John]
        System.out.println(copy);      // Student [id=1, name=John]

        original.setName("Jane");

        System.out.println(original);  // Student [id=1, name=Jane]
        System.out.println(copy);      // Student [id=1, name=Jane]
    }
}
```

As shown in the code, when the name of the original object is changed, the same change is reflected in the copied object. Shallow copy is useful when you want to create a new object with the same reference as the original object.

2. Deep Copy: Deep copy creates a new object and copies the values of the original object's fields into the new object. Both the original and copied objects refer to different memory locations, so changes made to the object will not affect each other.

Here's an example code demonstrating deep copy:

```java
class Student {
    private int id;
    private String name;

    public Student(int id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Student [id=" + id + ", name=" + name + "]";
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student original = new Student(1, "John");
        Student copy = new Student(original.getId(), original.getName());

        System.out.println(original);  // Student [id=1, name=John]
        System.out.println(copy);      // Student [id=1, name=John]

        original.setName("Jane");

        System.out.println(original);  // Student [id=1, name=Jane]
        System.out.println(copy);      // Student [id=1, name=John]
    }
}
```

In the code, even after changing the name of the original object, the copied object retains its original name. Deep copy is useful when you want to create a completely independent copy of an object.

The choice between deep copy and shallow copy depends on the requirements and characteristics of the objects being copied. If you want the copied object to be independent and not affected by changes made to the original object, deep copy is preferred. On the other hand, if you want both objects to refer to the same memory location and reflect changes made to each other, shallow copy is preferred.

