```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class CombinationCalculator {

    public static int calculateCombinations(List<Integer> items) {
        return calculateCombinationsHelper(items, new ArrayList<>(), 0);
    }

    private static int calculateCombinationsHelper(List<Integer> items, List<Integer> currentCombination, int count) {
        if (items.isEmpty()) {
            return count + 1;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < items.size(); i++) {
            List<Integer> newCombination = new ArrayList<>(currentCombination);
            newCombination.add(items.get(i));

            List<Integer> newItems = new ArrayList<>(items);
            newItems.remove(i);

            count = calculateCombinationsHelper(newItems, newCombination, count);
        }

        return count;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> items = new ArrayList<>();
        items.add(1);
        items.add(2);
        items.add(3);

        int numCombinations = calculateCombinations(items);
        System.out.println("Number of combinations: " + numCombinations);
    }
}
```

The `calculateCombinations` function is the entry point of the calculation and it takes a list of integers as input. It then calls the `calculateCombinationsHelper` function to perform the actual calculation.

The `calculateCombinationsHelper` function is a recursive function that takes three parameters: the list of items, the current combination being built, and the count of combinations found so far. It first checks if the list of items is empty, which means that a new combination has been completed. In this case, it increments the count and returns it.

If the list of items is not empty, the function loops through each item in the list. It creates a new combination by adding the current item to the current combination. It also creates a new list of items by removing the current item from the original list. Then, it recursively calls the `calculateCombinationsHelper` function with the new list of items and the new combination. Finally, it updates the count with the returned value.

