Here is the recursive function in Java to print out the Fibonacci sequence up to the Nth term and return the sum of all the even terms:

```java
public class FibonacciSequence {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 10; // Example input for the Nth term

        System.out.println("Fibonacci sequence up to " + n + "th term:");
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            System.out.print(fibonacci(i) + " ");
        }

        int sumEvenTerms = sumEvenFibonacci(n);
        System.out.println("\nSum of even terms in Fibonacci sequence up to " + n + "th term: " + sumEvenTerms);
    }

    public static int fibonacci(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) {
            return n;
        } else {
            return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2);
        }
    }

    public static int sumEvenFibonacci(int n) {
        int sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int fib = fibonacci(i);
            if (fib % 2 == 0) {
                sum += fib;
            }
        }
        return sum;
    }
}
```

In the `fibonacci` method, we use the recursive formula `fibonacci(n) = fibonacci(n-1) + fibonacci(n-2)`. This method has a time complexity of O(2^N) because for each call to `fibonacci(n)`, it recursively calls `fibonacci(n-1)` and `fibonacci(n-2)`.

The `sumEvenFibonacci` method iterates over the Fibonacci sequence up to the Nth term using a loop and checks if each term is even. If it is even, it adds it to the `sum` variable. Finally, it returns the sum of all the even terms in the Fibonacci sequence up to the Nth term.

Note: The time complexity of the `sumEvenFibonacci` method is O(N) because it iterates over the Fibonacci sequence up to the Nth term once.

