One approach to add items to an array in JavaScript while maintaining a constant time complexity is by using a circular buffer or a ring buffer.

A circular buffer is a fixed-size data structure that allows elements to be efficiently added and removed in constant time. The buffer consists of a fixed-size array and two pointers: one for the beginning of the buffer (head) and one for the end (tail).

Here is an example implementation:

```javascript
class CircularBuffer {
  constructor(size) {
    this.buffer = new Array(size);
    this.head = 0; // points to the oldest item
    this.tail = 0; // points to the next available position
    this.length = 0; // current number of items in the buffer
  }

  enqueue(item) {
    if (this.length === this.buffer.length) {
      // Buffer is full, overwrite the oldest item
      this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.buffer.length;
    } else {
      this.length++;
    }

    this.buffer[this.tail] = item;
    this.tail = (this.tail + 1) % this.buffer.length;
  }

  get(index) {
    if (index < 0 || index >= this.length) {
      throw new Error('Index out of bounds');
    }

    return this.buffer[(this.head + index) % this.buffer.length];
  }
}
```

With this circular buffer implementation, the `enqueue` method adds items to the buffer in constant time, regardless of the buffer size. The `get` method allows retrieving items from the buffer by index, also in constant time.

Example usage:

```javascript
const buffer = new CircularBuffer(5);
buffer.enqueue(1);
buffer.enqueue(2);
buffer.enqueue(3);
buffer.enqueue(4);
buffer.enqueue(5);

console.log(buffer.get(0)); // Output: 1
console.log(buffer.get(3)); // Output: 4
```

By using a circular buffer, the time complexity of adding items to the array remains constant, O(1), even when the array size increases.

