Here is a Swift program that removes duplicates from a given array without using any additional data structures:

```swift
func removeDuplicates(in array: inout [Int]) {
    let n = array.count
    guard n > 1 else {
        return
    }

    var tail = 1

    for i in 1..<n {
        var isDuplicate = false

        for j in 0..<tail {
            if array[i] == array[j] {
                isDuplicate = true
                break
            }
        }

        if !isDuplicate {
            array[tail] = array[i]
            tail += 1
        }
    }

    array.removeLast(n - tail)
}

// Example usage:
var numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 2, 5, 6, 1, 4]
removeDuplicates(in: &numbers)
print(numbers) // Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
```

This program uses two nested loops to iterate over the elements of the array. The outer loop iterates over each element from the second element to the last. The inner loop checks if the current element is a duplicate by comparing it with all the elements that have been added to the result array so far. If it finds a duplicate, it sets the `isDuplicate` flag to true and breaks out of the inner loop. If the element is not a duplicate, it adds it to the result array by assigning it to the position `tail` and increments `tail` by 1. Finally, it removes the extra elements from the original array by calling `array.removeLast(n - tail)`.

The time complexity of this program is O(n^2), where n is the size of the array, because in the worst case scenario, the inner loop has to compare each element with all the previous elements.

