Here is a JavaScript function that finds the length of the longest common substring between two given strings and returns the actual substring:

```javascript
function longestCommonSubstring(str1, str2) {
  const len1 = str1.length;
  const len2 = str2.length;
  let maxLength = 0;
  let endIndex = 0;

  // Initialize a 2D array to store the lengths of longest common suffixes of substrings
  const dp = Array(len1 + 1).fill(0).map(() => Array(len2 + 1).fill(0));

  for (let i = 1; i <= len1; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j <= len2; j++) {
      if (str1[i - 1] === str2[j - 1]) {
        dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
        if (dp[i][j] > maxLength) {
          maxLength = dp[i][j];
          endIndex = i - 1;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Extract the longest common substring using the endIndex
  const longestSubstring = str1.substr(endIndex - maxLength + 1, maxLength);

  return { length: maxLength, substring: longestSubstring };
}

// Example usage:
const str1 = "abcdef";
const str2 = "bcdfgh";

console.log(longestCommonSubstring(str1, str2));
```

This function uses dynamic programming to solve the problem. It iterates through each character of the two strings and checks if they are the same. If they are, it updates the length of the longest common substring ending at that position. It also keeps track of the maxLength and endIndex of the longest common substring. After the loops, it extracts the longest common substring using the endIndex.

The time complexity of this function is O(n * m), where n is the length of the first string and m is the length of the second string, as we are filling in a 2D array of size (n+1) * (m+1).

