Here's a JavaScript code snippet that adds two numbers with up to 10^6 digits using a custom algorithm optimized for performance and memory usage:

```javascript
function addLargeNumbers(num1, num2) {
  const len1 = num1.length;
  const len2 = num2.length;
  const maxLength = Math.max(len1, len2);
  const result = [];
  let carry = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < maxLength; i++) {
    const digit1 = i < len1 ? parseInt(num1[len1 - 1 - i]) : 0;
    const digit2 = i < len2 ? parseInt(num2[len2 - 1 - i]) : 0;
    const sum = digit1 + digit2 + carry;
    carry = Math.floor(sum / 10);
    result.unshift(sum % 10);
  }

  if (carry > 0) {
    result.unshift(carry);
  }

  return result.join('');
}

// Example usage
const num1 = '12345678901234567890';
const num2 = '98765432109876543210';
const sum = addLargeNumbers(num1, num2);
console.log(sum); // Output: 111111111011111111100
```

In this code, we iterate through the digits of the two numbers from the least significant digit to the most significant digit. We add the corresponding digits and any carry from the previous sum, and store the result digit in an array. We also keep track of any carry left after adding all digits. Finally, we convert the resulting array of digits into a string and return it as the sum.

This algorithm has a time complexity of O(max(N, M)), where N and M are the number of digits in the two input numbers. It uses a constant amount of memory, as the resulting sum is stored in an array.

