Polymorphism in programming refers to the ability of an object to take on multiple forms. It allows different objects to be treated as instances of a common superclass, even though they may have different specific implementations.

One way to implement polymorphism is through inheritance and method overriding. Inheritance allows a subclass to inherit the properties and methods of its superclass. Method overriding occurs when a subclass provides a different implementation for a method already defined in its superclass. This enables the subclass to use the same method name but with different behaviors.

Let's consider an example in the Java programming language. We'll create a banking application with different types of accounts (e.g., SavingsAccount, CheckingAccount), all inheriting from a common Account class.

```java
class Account {
  protected double balance;

  public void deposit(double amount) {
    balance += amount;
  }

  public void withdraw(double amount) {
    if (amount <= balance) {
      balance -= amount;
    } else {
      System.out.println("Insufficient funds");
    }
  }

  public double getBalance() {
    return balance;
  }
}

class SavingsAccount extends Account {
  // additional specific behaviors for savings accounts
}

class CheckingAccount extends Account {
  // additional specific behaviors for checking accounts
}

public class BankingApp {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Account savingsAccount = new SavingsAccount();
    Account checkingAccount = new CheckingAccount();

    savingsAccount.deposit(500);
    checkingAccount.deposit(1000);

    savingsAccount.withdraw(200);
    checkingAccount.withdraw(500);

    System.out.println("Savings Account Balance: " + savingsAccount.getBalance());
    System.out.println("Checking Account Balance: " + checkingAccount.getBalance());
  }
}
```

In this example, the Account class serves as the common superclass for both SavingsAccount and CheckingAccount. Each account type can have its specific behaviors or additional methods.

Polymorphic behavior is achieved through method overriding. Although both savingsAccount and checkingAccount are declared as instances of the Account class, they have their own specific implementations for the deposit, withdraw, and getBalance methods. When these methods are called on the objects, the appropriate implementation based on the actual object type is executed.

By using polymorphism, the banking application becomes more flexible and extensible. It allows us to treat different types of accounts uniformly, using a common interface (the Account class). If new account types are introduced in the future, we can simply create new subclasses of Account without modifying the existing code that interacts with accounts. This promotes code reuse, reduces code duplication, and enhances maintainability.

