Here's an example Java code that demonstrates creating multiple threads, each performing a specific task with multiple steps and using shared memory for communication. It also implements a priority-based scheduling algorithm to determine the order of execution.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

class Task implements Runnable {
    private static final Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    private static int sharedMemory = 0;
    private int priority;

    public Task(int priority) {
        this.priority = priority;
    }

    private void doStep1() {
        System.out.println("Thread " + Thread.currentThread().getName() + " executing step 1");
        // Perform step 1 logic
        sharedMemory += 1;
    }

    private void doStep2() {
        System.out.println("Thread " + Thread.currentThread().getName() + " executing step 2");
        // Perform step 2 logic
        sharedMemory += 2;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            doStep1();
            doStep2();
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public int getPriority() {
        return priority;
    }
}

class PriorityScheduler implements Runnable {
    private Task[] tasks;

    public PriorityScheduler(Task[] tasks) {
        this.tasks = tasks;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        // Sort tasks based on priority in descending order
        for (int i = 0; i < tasks.length - 1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < tasks.length - i - 1; j++) {
                if (tasks[j].getPriority() < tasks[j + 1].getPriority()) {
                    Task temp = tasks[j];
                    tasks[j] = tasks[j + 1];
                    tasks[j + 1] = temp;
                }
            }
        }

        // Execute tasks based on priority
        for (Task task : tasks) {
            Thread thread = new Thread(task);
            thread.start();

            try {
                thread.join();
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Task[] tasks = {
                new Task(2),
                new Task(1),
                new Task(3)
        };

        PriorityScheduler scheduler = new PriorityScheduler(tasks);
        Thread schedulerThread = new Thread(scheduler);
        schedulerThread.start();

        try {
            schedulerThread.join();
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        System.out.println("Final shared memory value: " + Task.sharedMemory);
    }
}
```

In this code, the `Task` class represents the individual tasks to be performed by each thread. Each task has a priority assigned to it. The `doStep1` and `doStep2` methods represent the multiple steps involved in the task, where some complex logical reasoning can be implemented.

The `run` method of the `Task` class is called when a thread executes the task. It acquires a lock using a `ReentrantLock` to ensure synchronization while accessing the shared memory. The steps of the task are then executed, updating the shared memory accordingly.

The `PriorityScheduler` class implements the priority-based scheduling algorithm. It sorts the tasks based on priority in descending order and then executes them in that order. Each task is executed in a separate thread, and the scheduler waits for each thread to complete using the `join` method.

In the `main` method, an array of tasks is created with different priorities. The `PriorityScheduler` is instantiated with these tasks and executed in a separate thread. Finally, the value of the shared memory is printed to verify the correctness of synchronization and communication between the threads.

Please note that this is a simplified example to demonstrate the concept. In real-world scenarios, you may need to handle more complex logic, error handling, and utilize more advanced synchronization mechanisms.

