Here is a procedure in PostgreSQL that will delete a table only if it exists. It will return an error message if the table does not exist and display a warning message if the table has any foreign key constraints.

```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE delete_table(table_name VARCHAR) AS $$
DECLARE
    table_exists BOOLEAN;
    fk_exists BOOLEAN;
BEGIN
    SELECT EXISTS (
        SELECT 1
        FROM information_schema.tables
        WHERE table_name = $1
    ) INTO table_exists;

    IF table_exists THEN
        SELECT EXISTS (
            SELECT 1
            FROM information_schema.table_constraints
            WHERE constraint_type = 'FOREIGN KEY'
            AND table_name = $1
        ) INTO fk_exists;

        IF fk_exists THEN
            RAISE WARNING 'Table % cannot be deleted due to existing foreign key constraints.', $1;
        ELSE
            EXECUTE 'DROP TABLE ' || quote_ident($1) || ';';
            RAISE NOTICE 'Table % has been deleted.', $1;
        END IF;
    ELSE
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Table % does not exist.', $1;
    END IF;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;
```

To use the procedure, simply call it with the name of the table you want to delete:

```sql
CALL delete_table('your_table_name');
```

If the table exists and has foreign key constraints, it will display a warning message. If the table does not exist, it will return an error message. If the table exists and does not have any foreign key constraints, it will be deleted and a notice message will be displayed.

