```java
public class PrimeNumberGenerator {
    private static final int CHUNK_SIZE = 1000;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int limit = 10000000; // maximum number to check

        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        boolean[] primes = sieveOfEratosthenes(limit);
        printPrimes(primes);
        long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

        System.out.println("Execution time: " + (endTime - startTime) + "ms");
    }

    private static boolean[] sieveOfEratosthenes(int limit) {
        int size = limit + 1;
        boolean[] primes = new boolean[size / 2];
        primes[0] = true;

        for (int i = 1; i * i <= limit; i++) {
            if (primes[i]) {
                int number = 2 * i + 1;
                for (int j = number * number; j <= limit; j += number) {
                    if (j % 2 == 1) {
                        primes[(j - 1) / 2] = false;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return primes;
    }

    private static void printPrimes(boolean[] primes) {
        int count = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < primes.length; i++) {
            if (primes[i]) {
                int number = 2 * i + 1;
                System.out.print(number + " ");
                count++;

                if (count % CHUNK_SIZE == 0) {
                    System.out.println();
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this program, the `sieveOfEratosthenes` method implements the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm to generate a boolean array `primes`, where `primes[i]` represents whether the number `2 * i + 1` is prime or not. The method iterates through the array starting from index 1 (which corresponds to number 3) and marks all multiples of each prime number as non-prime by setting their corresponding array elements to false.

The `printPrimes` method handles the printing of prime numbers. It iterates through the `primes` array and prints each prime number. To optimize the printing, it prints numbers in chunks of `CHUNK_SIZE` and adds a line break after each chunk.

The `main` method is the entry point of the program. It sets the maximum number to check (`limit`), calls the `sieveOfEratosthenes` method to generate the `primes` array, and then calls the `printPrimes` method to print the prime numbers. It also measures the execution time of the program.

Note that this program uses a bit array instead of a boolean array to optimize memory usage. In the `sieveOfEratosthenes` method, each boolean value is represented by a bit in the array, reducing the memory usage significantly. The index `i` in the array corresponds to the number `2 * i + 1`.

This implementation should be able to handle numbers up to 10 million efficiently without exceeding a time limit of 1 second, thanks to the optimized Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm with O(sqrt(n)) time complexity.

