```python
class Stack:
    def __init__(self):
        self.stack = []
        self.min_stack = []

    def push(self, value):
        self.stack.append(value)
        if not self.min_stack or value <= self.min_stack[-1]:
            self.min_stack.append(value)

    def pop(self):
        if not self.is_empty():
            value = self.stack.pop()
            if value == self.min_stack[-1]:
                self.min_stack.pop()
            return value

    def top(self):
        if not self.is_empty():
            return self.stack[-1]

    def is_empty(self):
        return len(self.stack) == 0

    def size(self):
        return len(self.stack)

    def get_min(self):
        if not self.is_empty():
            return self.min_stack[-1]

    def peek_min(self):
        return self.get_min()
```

In this implementation, we have used two stacks - one to store the elements of the stack and another one to keep track of the minimum element at each step. Whenever we push an element onto the stack, we also update the minimum stack if the new element is smaller than or equal to the current minimum. This way, we can always access the minimum element in O(1) time complexity by peeking at the top of the minimum stack.

